CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is clear, but its real observable effect requires a save path to be reached and depends on the runtime contents and validity of each installation's settings files and environment-variable resolution results.

CLI settings persistence now reads and updates existing comment-bearing JSON instead of replacing it with freshly serialized JSON. It also records environment-variable references during settings loading and attempts to restore those references when a scope is saved, so resolved values are not normally written to disk. Invalid existing settings files are now left unchanged by this writer.

Likely behavioral changes
  • Saving an existing valid settings file now updates its parsed structure and serializes it with comment-aware tooling, retaining comments and existing structure while applying supplied setting values.
  • For settings whose original values were complete environment-variable references and resolved to different strings, saving the same scope now substitutes the original references back into the persisted structure rather than normally exposing the resolved values.
  • If an existing settings file cannot be parsed, this save path reports the parse problem and returns without changing the file; a missing file is still created as ordinary formatted JSON.

Investigate
  • Trace all callers that modify or save settings, especially reset, unset, migration, and bulk-update flows, to determine whether they rely on omitted properties being removed from disk.
  • Inspect environment-variable resolution across objects, arrays, dotted property names, unresolved variables, and values deliberately changed after loading.
  • Review file-write guarantees and comment-json behavior, including metadata preservation, concurrent edits, symlinks, partial writes, and malformed JSON handling.
  • Identify which CLI commands, interactive settings surfaces, or library entry points reach these functions and which setting scopes they can modify.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not identify which CLI commands, settings interfaces, migrations, or external library callers invoke LoadedSettings.setValue or saveSettings.
  • It is not established whether settings persistence is exposed only through CLI interaction or also through other callable application surfaces.
  • Observable impact depends on whether real settings files contain comments, complete environment-variable references, arrays containing references, malformed JSON, or properties that callers intend to delete.
  • Filesystem permissions, concurrent modifications, and failure behavior during the non-atomic synchronous write cannot be established from the supplied diff.
  • The exact preservation guarantees and serialization behavior of comment-json 4.2.5, including comment attachment and formatting normalization for all supported JSON-with-comments constructs, are not established here.

